WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.816
Background work still needs access control.

2
00:00:02.816 --> 00:00:14.145
A worker may not have an interactive user at the moment it runs, but it often acts on behalf of a user, a tenant, an administrator, a scheduled policy, or a service process.

3
00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:22.986
The application should know which category applies because it affects authorization, audit records, error handling, and accountability.

4
00:00:22.986 --> 00:00:27.308
Authentication and authorization are different in worker contexts.

5
00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:33.529
A service identity proves which workload is calling a database, queue, storage account, or API.

6
00:00:33.529 --> 00:00:42.567
Authorization decides whether this specific operation should occur on this specific object for this specific requester, tenant, or policy.

7
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:47.937
A valid ExampleServiceIdentity should not automatically mean every job is allowed.

8
00:00:47.937 --> 00:00:54.878
Object-level authorization matters for records, files, reports, exports, jobs, and administrative actions.

9
00:00:54.878 --> 00:00:59.790
A worker may be allowed to generate reports, but not every tenant's report.

10
00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:07.190
It may be allowed to process imports, but only for jobs that are approved and still owned by the expected tenant.

11
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:12.363
Access checks should follow the object and operation, not only the worker role.

12
00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:15.114
Tenant boundaries deserve explicit checks.

13
00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:18.715
A tenant identifier in a message is a claim, not proof.

14
00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:28.538
The worker should verify ownership against authoritative data and preserve tenant context throughout processing, storage, logging, and external calls.

15
00:01:28.538 --> 00:01:37.445
Cross-tenant operations, support actions, migrations, and repairs should receive stronger review because mistakes can affect many users.

16
00:01:37.445 --> 00:01:42.356
User-initiated jobs and system-initiated jobs need different audit context.

17
00:01:42.356 --> 00:01:49.691
A user-initiated ExampleImportJob should preserve the requester, approver if needed, tenant, job ID, and reason.

18
00:01:49.691 --> 00:01:56.632
A system-initiated cleanup job should preserve the service identity, policy, schedule, and affected scope.

19
00:01:56.632 --> 00:02:01.413
Audit trails should explain why the worker acted, not only that it acted.

20
00:02:01.413 --> 00:02:07.438
Privileged workers should not perform actions that the requester was not allowed to trigger.

21
00:02:07.438 --> 00:02:17.261
This is the defensive version of a confused-deputy problem: a trusted worker can unintentionally lend its broader access to a lower-privilege request.

22
00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:26.495
Administrative, support, cleanup, migration, and repair jobs should be reviewed as high-impact paths with clear approval, scope, and logging.
